// @run
// @log 7
// @log 9
// @hint redundant type annotation: `mkI` is inferred as `i32`
// A GENERIC function whose parameter is a ZERO-PARAMETER closure (`f: () => T`).
// Monomorphization takes the pattern annotation apart three ways — "does it carry a
// type param" (`monoAnnHasTyParam`), "substitute the binding through it"
// (`monoSubstAnn`) and "bind the params from the actual argument type"
// (`monoBindFromAnn`) — and each decomposes a function type into its PARAM LIST and
// its RETURN.
//
// The empty param list is the seam. `annSplitParams("")` pushes ONE EMPTY part, so a
// decomposition that splits an empty inner list hands `monoSubstAnn` a `""` parameter,
// which reports "unbound type param" for the WHOLE annotation — and the instance is
// then unnameable (`emitProgram: monomorphize: unsupported argument type for 'f' in a
// call to 'callIt'`). Two of the three copies guarded on `innerFn.length > 0` and the
// third did not; the one home (`annFnDecompose`, D-GRAMHOME) carries the guard, and
// this is the case that discriminates it.
//
// No corpus program reached it before: every generic closure parameter in the tree
// takes at least one argument, so dropping the guard was inert on 1,314 corpus files
// AND on 50,400 fuzz programs (the generator emits no generic declarations at all).
// Covered at both result reps a zero-param closure can bind — the i32 result and a
// DISTINCT-valtype i64 result, which is what proves `T` really came from the return.
function callIt<T>(f: () => T): T {
  const a = f()
  a
}
function mkI(): i32 { 7 }
function mkL(): i64 { 9 }
print(callIt(mkI))
print(callIt(mkL))
